{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789465827,"nonce":"9ukZ+rdHHQCuRPjj0Pkgi2ekJp8=","title":"One aggregate calling convention for the Cranelift and LLVM tiers","message":"The tiers lower a function signature independently. Cranelift (`translate_signature_for`, `translate_type`): a struct parameter is a pointer to its storage, a struct of one scalar field is that scalar, a `List`/`Array` header is returned as a pointer, any other struct return goes through a caller-provided destination that leads the parameters (`returns_through_destination`), except for address-taken functions. LLVM (`declare_function`, `translate_type`): `HirType::Struct` is an LLVM struct by value in parameters and returns.\n\nInside one tier this is invisible. It shows at every edge where code of one tier calls code of the other: a promoted root whose signature carries an aggregate is published into a bead that Cranelift callers use (latent today), and per-function LLVM promotion (107df3c) makes LLVM callers of Cranelift callees the normal case. Ash never had this problem because HashLink's ABI has no by-value aggregates; both of its tiers emit one `AbiClass` per parameter.\n\nDecision: the convention is Cranelift's, stated once in `crates/compiler/src/abi.rs` (or equivalent) as a function of the HIR signature, and both backends derive their declarations, call sites, parameter binding and returns from it. The LLVM side changes: aggregate parameters arrive as pointers (loaded to the first-class value the body uses, or used as the address where the body treats the value as one), aggregate returns store through the destination, call sites materialise storage for aggregate arguments and pass its address, and the same single-scalar and list-header carve-outs apply. The host entry path (`call_native_with_signature`) already follows Cranelift's rules and is unaffected.\n\nVerify with a ZynML program whose functions pass and return structs, run with both tiers and with the LLVM tier calling a Cranelift-compiled callee once 107df3c lands; `ZYNTAX_DUMP_LLVM_IR` and `ZYNTAX_DUMP_CRANELIFT` show the two declarations side by side.","files":null}]}